# /etc/crawler/crawler.env — the EnvironmentFile of all Go services
# (deploy/systemd/*.service, docs/deploy.md). Installation:
# cp deploy/systemd/crawler.env.example /etc/crawler/crawler.env &&
# chmod 0640 && chown root:crawler.
#
# This is ONLY the environment entry point: the configuration structure is
# a single YAML (CRAWLER_CONFIG → /etc/crawler/config.yaml from
# config.example.yaml; the env > YAML > defaults priority —
# docs/configuration.md). The secret (DSN) is kept here, not in YAML: the
# file is 0640 root:crawler and never lands in git.
# The values below are a sample — replace them with production ones.

# The single configuration YAML (required).
CRAWLER_CONFIG=/etc/crawler/config.yaml

# PostgreSQL: the role/DB is created by the installation (docs/deploy.md);
# sslmode=disable — loopback of a single server (spec 3.1).
CRAWLER_POSTGRES_DSN=postgres://crawler:CHANGE_ME@127.0.0.1:5432/crawler?sslmode=disable

# The PostgreSQL connection pool PER ONE service: the spec budget of 20–50
# is the total across all of them (8 services × 5 + dashboard 2 from its
# unit + crawlerctl 2 + admin ≈ 44–46 at max_connections=50). Enabled, this
# variable would override the dashboard pool too (EnvironmentFile beats the
# unit's Environment): a safe global maximum is 4 (9 × 4 + 2 + admin ≈ 40).
# The config.yaml example value already fits; uncomment to set it for all
# services at once (the variable beats YAML — docs/configuration.md).
#CRAWLER_POSTGRES_POOL_SIZE=4

# Redis and the local resolver (spec 5.4: the local unbound only;
# fallback resolvers — dns.fallback_resolvers in the config).
CRAWLER_REDIS_ADDR=127.0.0.1:6379
CRAWLER_DNS_RESOLVERS=127.0.0.1:53

# The external Chromium — the crawler-chromium unit (CDP loopback;
# docs/deploy.md: why the browser is a separate unit, not a worker process).
CRAWLER_RENDER_REMOTE_URL=ws://127.0.0.1:9222

# The crawler's User-Agent (spec §9: honest, with the operator's real
# contacts — replacing it is mandatory; in dev it carries a dev tag, this
# one is production).
CRAWLER_HTTP_USER_AGENT=DomainCrawler/1.0 (+https://crawler.example.com/contact; admin@example.com)

# The SSRF circuit (T-219/T-230): filter internal addresses (loopback/
# RFC1918/ULA/link-local, including IPv4-in-IPv6) on all paths — the DNS
# stage addresses, redirects, literals and render requests; the render
# interception also resolves hostnames (DNS rebinding over time is closed
# inside Chromium itself; the residual vector — an attacker telling two
# resolvers apart — is held by the egress policy of the Chromium
# container). A production (Internet) deploy must enable it: the DB, Redis
# and the resolver live on the network next to the crawler. Disabling (0)
# is only a deliberate choice for a dev/testbed with internal networks.
# It duplicates the true of the config.yaml example: env beats YAML, so a
# production deploy keeps the circuit enabled through any YAML edit.
CRAWLER_HTTP_BLOCK_INTERNAL_ADDRS=1

# monitor alert delivery to Telegram (T-162, spec 5.9/10.4; monitor.alerts.
# telegram in the config): the same bot and dedicated group as the CI/CD
# notifications (docs/ci-cd.md). The token — @BotFather; the group id — the
# numeric id of the chat the bot was added to (getUpdates after the first
# message). Both are required: missing either — monitor skips sending
# (alerts stay Warn in journald, just like a CI step stays silent without
# secrets). The sending script also takes them from the environment and
# does not print them: the token never lands in logs or messages.
#TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
#TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=

# The Telegram delivery proxy (if the production IP cannot reach
# api.telegram.org — RKN blocking; the telegram-notify.sh script's curl
# picks it up automatically). An HTTP proxy on an external VPS: CONNECT
# 443 only, Allow only that server's IP — installation and rollback:
# docs/operations.md §4.3 item 3 and §5 (the Oct 24 entry). Left commented
# out — uncomment for this network layout.
#https_proxy=http://104.253.1.37:3128
